<h1>Running Folder Parameter test</h1>
<p>Unlike the <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/running-hello-world-test/">Hello World test</a>, this test
  can't just be run as-is: its <code>maze_executable_folder</code> parameter is
  <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/adding-configuration-parameter/#config-class">required</a> and has no
  meaningful default, so a value has to be supplied before the test can do anything.</p>

<h2 id="running-from-cli">Running from CLI</h2>
<p>Running the test file as-is fails, because the required folder was never set:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("python test_folder_parameter.py") }}
<p>To run it from the CLI, pass a config with the folder you downloaded the Maze application into. Adjust the
  test's <code>main</code> function like this:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def main() -> None:
	test = TestFolderParameter(
		TestFolderParameterConfig(
			maze_executable_folder=r"C:\\Orcraft\\Maze\\bin\\v3.0.0",
		)
	)
	test.run()') }}
<p>This is exactly what the controller does for you when you pick a folder in the GUI - it constructs the test's
  config with the values you chose.</p>

<h3 id="ansi-output">ANSI output</h3>
<p>With a valid folder, the test runs the Maze executable and logs its output as part of the test's own log:</p>
{% ansiOutput "folder-parameter-run.ansi" %}
<p>Notice the <strong>Maze Executable Folder</strong> column in the configuration table - every config parameter
  a test declares shows up there, so the log always records exactly what the test was run with. The indented
  block in the middle is the Maze application's own stdout, captured and logged by the test.</p>

<h2 id="running-from-gui">Running from GUI</h2>
<p>From the controller's GUI there's no code to edit: select the <strong>Folder Parameter</strong> test, and its
  configuration tab now shows the <strong>Maze Executable Folder</strong> field alongside the built-in
  <code>Cycles</code> field we met earlier. Pick the version folder holding the Maze executable, then click
  <strong class="text-purple">Run</strong>.</p>
<p>Since the field is <code>required</code>, the run is blocked until a folder is selected - an empty value is
  reported as a configuration error rather than a test failure.</p>
